<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Non-cooperation movement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Non-Cooperation movement</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Non-cooperation movement was a mass campaign led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> between 1920 and 1922 against British rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Launched after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rowlatt-act">Rowlatt Act</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jallianwala-bagh-massacre">Jallianwala Bagh massacre</a> and in alliance with the Khilafat movement, it called on Indians to boycott British courts, schools, councils and goods. Gandhi suspended it after the violence at Chauri Chaura in February 1922.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/non-cooperation-movement.jpg" alt="Non-cooperation movement" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: British Information Services, an agency of the British Government, restoration by Wilfredor · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:India,_by_British_Information_Services,_1944_restored_version.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
